# web2-admin backend environment. Copy to apps/web2-admin/backend/.env and edit.
# Loaded by `import 'dotenv/config'` (see src/utils/config.ts) AND by docker
# compose, both for ${VAR} interpolation and via `env_file:`.
#
# Two run modes:
#   * host      pnpm database:start && pnpm dev      (API on the host, :9877)
#   * dockered  docker compose -p web2-admin --profile full up -d --build
#
# Anything marked "required" makes the process exit at startup when missing.

# --- Postgres ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Interpolated into the postgres container's POSTGRES_PASSWORD and into the
# api service's DATABASE_URL. Any string; it never leaves this compose project.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=web2admin

# Required. Connection string the API uses.
#   - host mode, against `pnpm database:start` (published on 5433, not 5432, so
#     it does not collide with streaming-infra-manager's postgres):
#       postgres://web2admin:<password>@127.0.0.1:5433/web2admin
#   - docker mode: docker-compose.yml overrides this to reach the container by
#     service name, so the value below only applies in host mode. Keep in sync.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://web2admin:web2admin@127.0.0.1:5433/web2admin

# --- HTTP -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. Listen port. Default: 9877 (the manager API uses 9876).
WEB2_ADMIN_PORT=9877

# Optional. Bind address. Default: 0.0.0.0.
WEB2_ADMIN_HOST=0.0.0.0

# Optional, server deployments only (deploy/deploy.sh). Loopback port the
# nginx console is published on when no --portSlot is given. Default: 9090
# (the manager's console is 8080). Ignored in host mode and when a slot is set.
WEB2_ADMIN_WEB_PORT=9090

# --- Sessions ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing to configure. A session lasts twelve hours of inactivity and fourteen
# days at most, both fixed in @streaming-monorepo/web2-admin-common so the
# console and the API cannot disagree about them. The cookie's Secure flag is
# decided per request from the first X-Forwarded-Proto hop (or a direct TLS
# connection), not from a variable: a Secure cookie handed out over plain HTTP
# is dropped by the browser and the sign-in loops.
#
# SESSION_TTL_HOURS and COOKIE_SECURE used to live here and are now ignored.

# --- The first user ---------------------------------------------------------
# There is no seeded account and no sign-up route, deliberately: a password in
# a file is a password, and a default nobody changes is the first thing tried.
# A fresh database has no users, and every sign-in answers 401 no_users until
# one is made with the CLI, which prompts twice with echo off:
#
#   pnpm user:add alice
#   docker compose exec -it api node dist/cli.js user:add alice
#
# Or from a vault, with the value never reaching a file or an argv:
#
#   op read "op://<vault>/<item>/password" \
#     | docker compose exec -T api node dist/cli.js user:add alice --password-stdin
#
# The first user ever added can manage users whatever the flags said. Later
# ones are plain unless --admin is given. SEED_ADMIN_USERNAME and
# SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD are gone; if they are still in your .env they are ignored.

# --- Swarm ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. `bee` writes the stream list feed through a real Bee node; `fake`
# swaps in an in-memory gateway that records feed writes and returns plausible
# references without touching Swarm — for local UI work and the integration
# tests. Default: bee.
#
# There is no Bee node or batch to set here. The catalogue is written through
# the catalogue node and batch the manager designates and pushes into this
# admin (PUT /api/internal/catalogue-stamp), read on every write; until it has
# pushed one, `bee` refuses to publish and says why. `fake` needs none.
# BEE_URL and POSTAGE_BATCH_ID are no longer read; an env file that still sets
# them starts as it did, and the boot log names them.
FEED_GATEWAY=bee

# Required. 0x + 64 hex, and a valid secp256k1 key — startup derives its
# address and refuses the value if it cannot. That address is the `owner` of
# the stream list feed, and of a stream with no stage, and is what viewers use
# to find the feed. A stream on a stage has its stage's owner.
# Treat it as a secret: it is the identity of the whole catalog.
#
# DO NOT hand this key to a running swarm-hls-stream uploader. That process
# keeps its own idea of the feed's next index, and two writers at one index
# fork the feed.
#
# Empty on purpose, and the API refuses to start until it is set. A key
# printed in a public file signs a catalog anyone can write. Generate your own,
# for development as much as for a real catalog:
#   node -e "console.log('0x'+require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
FEED_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Optional. Raw (pre-hash) topic of the stream list feed. Default: swarm-stream,
# which is what swarm-hls-stream and msrs-client both read.
FEED_TOPIC=swarm-stream

# Optional. Base URL of the branded viewer, for "open player catalogue" links;
# the frontend opens its root (#/), which lists the catalogue feed the viewer
# was built for. That viewer must therefore be built for THIS backend's feed
# owner and topic (GET /api/config). A stream's own route,
# #/watch/<mediatype>/<owner>/<topic>, only plays after the uploader has
# written a first segment, so drafts never link straight to it. Empty
# disables the link. Differs per brand and per deployment,
# e.g. https://player.example.com.
VIEWER_BASE_URL=

# --- Internal API (what the manager calls) ----------------------------------
# Required, at least 32 characters. The registrar token: the bearer token the
# manager pushes stage and catalogue stamp records with on /api/internal, and
# the one its web2 admin link stores. A session cookie is never accepted there
# and this token is never accepted anywhere else. No stream uploader is given
# it: each presents a token of its own, which the manager generates and tells
# this admin about by its sha256, and the uploader's routes refuse this one.
#
# It can register a stage and designate the catalogue's batch, so treat it as a
# secret and keep the API off the public internet. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# and type the same value into the manager's Manager settings, Web2 admin link.
INTERNAL_API_TOKEN=change-me-to-32-or-more-random-characters

# --- Moving the catalogue to another batch ----------------------------------
# Off unless set to true. With it on, an operator can move the catalogue's
# history onto the batch the manager designated, from the Stages page: every
# slot of the feed is uploaded again under that batch before the admin writes
# with it. It stays off on an installation until the move has been tried on a
# real node (docs/architecture/stages.md, "Trying the move on a real node").
# CATALOGUE_MOVE_ENABLED=false

# --- Ingest (what the operator types into OBS) ------------------------------
# Nothing to set. Each stream's OBS details come from the stage it is
# broadcast on, as the manager pushes that stage: its public ingest address,
# its SRT and RTMP ports, whether RTMP is open, and its SRT passphrase. The
# INGEST_* keys this file used to carry are no longer read, and the boot log
# names any that an env file still sets.
